
# 01 — Business Understanding
## World Vision Australia — Donor Retention Analytics

> **Business question:** How can World Vision Australia (WVA) use first-gift information to identify priority supporters, inform re-engagement timing and make follow-up communication more relevant?

This project was completed as a client-based capstone for **MIS779 — Decision Analytics in Practice** at Deakin University, using client-supplied data and mentor support to develop evidence-informed retention recommendations.

---

## Business Findings at a Glance

The final analysis examined progression from a first qualifying one-off giving occasion to another qualifying one-off gift within **24 months**.

| Key finding | Business implication |
|---|---|
| **16.6% repeat rate** — 3,194 of 19,194 eligible supporters made another qualifying one-off gift within 24 months. | Investigate opportunities to improve progression, while recognising that other forms of supporter engagement fall outside this measure. |
| **Top-20% prioritisation** — The highest-ranked 20% recorded a 23.2% repeat rate versus the 13.8% test-cohort baseline, capturing 33.7% of repeat donors. | Use propensity ranking to identify groups for controlled tests of additional retention attention. |
| **75.7% returned within 12 months** — Among 3,194 supporters who repeated within 24 months. | Investigate first-year engagement periods as candidates for re-engagement experiments. |
| **Retail Stand: 3.7% vs Website: 17.0%** — Observed repeat rates differed by acquisition channel. | Review Retail Stand onboarding, supporter contact details and follow-up processes before testing changes. |

**Population note:** The 16.6% overall repeat rate and channel comparison describe the broader descriptive cohort of 19,194 supporters. Predictive results describe a separate final Mass test cohort of 6,741 supporters, with a 13.8% repeat baseline. The timing finding describes only supporters who repeated within 24 months.

These findings identify opportunities for investigation and testing. They do not establish that additional contact causes more donations.

